PART 1: USER ACCOUNT ISOLATION AND CARD PERSISTENCE

1. Fix Card Leakage Between User Accounts

Current problem

When one user logs out and another user logs in, the second user can see the cards used by the previous user. This is incorrect and must be fixed.

Required behavior

Every user's cards must be isolated by the authenticated user's unique ID.

Each user's generated cards must be associated with the correct user ID in the database.

When a user logs in, load only that user's saved cards.

When the user logs out, clear the previous user's card data from client-side application state and any user-specific caches.

When another user logs in, initialize the interface using that user's own saved cards.

Never display another user's cards simply because they exist in the same game or browser session.

Ensure that switching accounts does not reuse stale card data from React state, local storage, session storage, cached API responses, or other client-side persistence.

Enforce ownership on the backend. A user must not be able to retrieve, edit, delete, register, or claim another user's private cards by changing an ID in an API request.

Use the authenticated user's identity from the trusted authentication session. Do not trust a user ID supplied by the frontend as the sole authorization mechanism.

Acceptance criteria

User A logs in and creates five cards.

User A logs out.

User B logs in using the same device.

User B sees only cards belonging to User B.

User A's cards remain safely stored and are available again when User A logs back in.

User B cannot access User A's cards through the UI or backend APIs.

2. Implement Correct Daily Initialization and Card Persistence

Required behavior

When a user opens the game for the first time during a new calendar day, the main game screen must not automatically display cards from previous days.

However, previously generated cards must remain saved and recoverable if the user has not deleted them.

Implement the following behavior:

First visit of the day

Show the main game interface without automatically displaying previous cards.

Keep previously saved cards in the database.

Do not delete or overwrite saved cards.

Do not automatically register any card.

Provide a clear way to access saved cards from previous games or days.

Returning later on the same day

Restore the user's active card display and applicable current-day state.

Keep cards the user has not deleted.

Restore valid registered cards and their registration statuses from the database.

Accessing previous cards

Provide a separate saved-cards or card-history section.

Allow the user to retrieve previously saved cards when needed.

Restore the correct ownership, registration status, and game association.

Never automatically register a previously generated or previously used card simply because it is restored.

Important distinction

Separate these concepts in the data model:

Card creation date.

Card owner.

Game association.

Card registration status.

Card display or active-session state.

Card deletion status.

Do not use a single local-storage flag to determine all of these states.

Use the application's configured timezone to determine the beginning of a calendar day. Store timestamps consistently, preferably in UTC, and calculate the applicable day boundary using the configured timezone.

Do not create a new game automatically just because a new day begins. Daily initialization should control the user's card display and session state, not override the host's authoritative game lifecycle.

3. Remove Automatic Card Registration

Current problem

The first card appears to be registered automatically. This must be corrected.

Required behavior

Generating a card and registering a card must be two separate actions.

When a user generates one or more cards:

Create the cards.

Save them under the authenticated user's account.

Display them as unregistered.

Show a clear Register Card button on each eligible card.

A card must only become registered when the user explicitly clicks Register Card and the backend confirms successful registration.

Do not automatically register:

The first generated card.

Cards generated in a batch.

Cards restored from previous sessions.

Cards loaded from saved history.

Cards restored after logging out and logging back in.

If registration is already closed or the game has started, prevent registration and explain why.

Use an atomic backend transaction to prevent two users from registering the same card.

Display the appropriate registration status on each card:

Not Registered.

Registered.

Registration Failed.

Already Registered by Another Player.

Blocked, where applicable.

Do not change the original owner of a registered card.

