PART 3: GAME START COUNTDOWN

5. Add a 15-Second Pre-Game Countdown

Required behavior

When an authorized host clicks Start Game, do not begin calling numbers immediately.

Instead, initiate a server-controlled pre-game countdown.

Display a prominent message on the player and host interfaces:

"The game is starting in 15 seconds, get ready!"

Display a live countdown:

15, 14, 13, 12 ... 3, 2, 1.

Countdown workflow

The host clicks Start Game.

The backend validates the game configuration and host permissions.

The backend atomically changes the game into a pre-start state.

Registration closes.

The selected winning rule and game configuration are locked.

The backend records the countdown start time and scheduled deadline.

All connected players receive the countdown event.

The player interface displays the message and remaining time.

The host interface displays the same countdown.

After 15 seconds, the countdown finishes.

The game transitions to In Progress.

Automatic number calling begins after an additional five-second delay.

Use a server-authoritative deadline rather than relying on individual clients' local countdown timers.

Clients should calculate the displayed remaining time from the authoritative server timestamp and synchronize with the backend as necessary.

Countdown cancellation and reliability

Prevent duplicate countdowns when the Start Game button is clicked multiple times.

Do not create multiple background timers.

Do not allow ordinary players to start or cancel the countdown.

If the host cancels the countdown before gameplay begins, stop the scheduled number-calling job and return to an appropriate pre-game state.

If the server restarts during the countdown, restore the countdown from its saved deadline.

Ensure that all clients display the same countdown deadline.

The five-second delay after the countdown must also be server-controlled.

Do not start the number-calling scheduler until that delay has expired.

PART 4: BINGO CLAIM WINDOW AND WINNER TRANSPARENCY

6. Implement a 15-Second Winner Claim Window

Current problem

The application should not immediately celebrate the first card that claims Bingo. A single player may have multiple registered cards, and other players must have a fair opportunity to submit their claims.

Required behavior

When the backend receives the first valid Bingo claim, initiate a 15-second claim window.

Display this message to all connected players:

"A winner has been found! Claim Bingo within 15 seconds before the game is closed."

Display a visible countdown timer:

15, 14, 13 ... 3, 2, 1.

Use the wording above for the initial notification, but clearly distinguish between a preliminary claim and a confirmed winner.

Important: Do not confirm the winner prematurely

The first valid claim must trigger the claim window, not an immediate final winner announcement.

During the 15-second window:

Keep the game in a winner-verification or claim-window state.

Allow other eligible players to submit claims using their other registered cards.

Allow the same player to claim Bingo with multiple registered cards.

Validate every claim independently against the official game history and the active winning rule.

Prevent duplicate submissions for the same card from creating duplicate claim records.

Record the server timestamp for each submitted claim.

Show an appropriate notification to all connected players.

A player must not be able to claim another player's card.

Claim-window rules

The first valid claim starts the authoritative 15-second deadline.

The server records the claim and its timestamp.

All connected clients receive the same deadline.

Additional eligible claims submitted within the window must be evaluated and recorded.

Claims that do not satisfy the winning rule must not be treated as winners.

A false claim must follow the application's false-claim blocking policy.

When the deadline expires, stop accepting new claims for that game.

Finalize the eligible winner list and complete the game.

Define the boundary precisely: a claim is eligible if it reaches the backend before the authoritative deadline and satisfies the verification rules.

Process claims and the closing operation transactionally so that a request arriving near the deadline cannot create inconsistent results.

Multiple winning cards

The game must support multiple winning cards in the same game.

For example:

Player A claims with Card 102.

Player A also claims with Card 105.

Player B claims with Card 208.

If all three cards independently satisfy the active winning rule and their claims are accepted within the window, record all three as winners.

Do not restrict the winner list to one card or one user unless the game's configured rules explicitly require that behavior.

Prevent premature celebrations

During the countdown, display a neutral message such as:

"Valid Bingo claim received. The claim window is open."

Do not display the final winner celebration until:

The claim window closes.

All eligible claims have been processed.

The final winners have been determined.

Once finalized, show a clear game-completion announcement.

Reliability requirements

The claim-window deadline must be stored on the server.

Client timers must be display-only.

The claim window must survive browser refreshes, disconnects, and server restarts.

Duplicate start events must not create multiple claim windows.

The closing operation must be idempotent.

No claim may be accepted after the authoritative deadline.

